.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1235 PM PDT Mon Oct 19 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

Morning marine layer mixed out giving way to mostly sunny skies
this afternoon. Despite the increased insolation temperatures are
much colder than yesterday. Definitely a far cry from last weeks
heat. 24 hour trends this afternoon are 5 to 20 degrees colder
with highs still on track to top out in the 60s to 70s (80s
interior Central Coast).

Tonight through Tuesday afternoon will be the proverbial calm
before the "storm". If we take a broader view of the EPac and
West Coast one can see an active pattern with change in our
sensible weather literally on the horizon. A robust 150kt upper
level jet is diving southward from the Gulf of Alaska and rounding
the base of an upper level trough off the PacNW as we speak. The
cyclonic curvature of the jet is supporting upper level
divergence, which in turn promotes surface cyclongensis. Or in
this case, will further enhance the surface low already positioned
off the PacNW. This surface low an associated upper level low are
all upstream. In the meantime, it`s a waiting game. Not expecting
a marine layer tonight due to increasing surface pressure
gradients and some marginal cold air advection. Don`t be surprised
to see some radar echoes off the N Bay Coast either today through
Tuesday. These radar returns are elevated and tied to the upper
jet max rounding the base of the digging trough.

For Tuesday the upper level trough deepens enough and eventually
becomes cut-off from the mean flow. Any weather watchers know that
upper level lows are tricky and can lead to lower confidence on
timing/position since they`re cut-off from the mean flow. For this
upper low, confidence begins to decrease as some hi-res guidance
begins to suggest two upper level vort maxes pivoting/rounding the
base of the upper trough. With two vort maxes focus of best
lift/dynamics becomes less certain. That being said, will carry a
chance of scattered showers over the coastal waters through
Tuesday afternoon. As for land locations, dry through Tuesday
afternoon with seasonably cool temps: 60s and 70s.

Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, enough moisture and lift
will be present with the nearby fully cut-off low to promote
showers moving onshore. Still best dynamics remain offshore, but a
few scout showers moving inland are plausible.

