.LONG TERM...
Issued at 130 PM PDT Mon Oct 19 2026
(Wednesday through next Sunday)

Wednesday will be an active day across the region as the upper
level low wobbles off the Central Coast before moving inland late
in the day. Solid upper level jet will fuel the main thrust with
this system. In fact, one could even argue a little bit of phasing
just offshore and through Point Conception during the day. As
such, expect precip chc to become more widespread across the
forecast area, but still main focus over the Central Coast. We`ve
been advertising a chance (15-20%) of thunderstorms over the
Central Coast waters and Central Coast. Current forecast keeps
the low chance in the forecast. Taking a closer look at dynamics
(still 48hrs out) this could end up being a lower CAPE high shear
environment. So what does that mean? Possible low topped cells
with some rotation. Given a solid lower level jet (under 850mb)
we`ll see a higher shear environment for CA standards. Combine
the shear with some meager SFC and MU CAPE developed storms may
rotate. Predicted hodographs are also elongated implying a deeper
layer of shear. Greatest likelihood is near the Big Sur Coast
Wednesday from 12-18Z. SPC does at least have this region in a
general mention. Given the convective threat we`ll be watching the
Timber and Plaskett burn areas. If a developed cell moves over
the right location it`s within the realm of possbilities that a
threshold will be met for debris flows. Very low conf high impact
scenario, but wanted to at least mention it. The upper level low
slowly loses upper level jet support and ultimately becomes an
open wave late Wednesday into Thursday. As such, we`ll keep precip
chances in the forecast through Thursday. Rainfall amounts from
Tuesday night through Thursday will generally be 0.05-0.25" and
0.25-0.75" Central Coast.

Drier weather returns by Thursday night as the now open wave moves
eastward. Behind the departing system some form of ridging
develops with a warming and drying trend heading into the
weekend. Not expecting the heat we just had, but a few degrees
above normal will be possible.

